WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.292
This is the executive briefing
for Book 08 of the Federal

3
00:00:05.292 --> 00:00:10.112
Modernization Workbook: DNS,
DHCP and IP address management.

4
00:00:10.112 --> 00:00:15.801
It's for the CIO, the CISO, the network
and infrastructure lead, and sponsors.

5
00:00:15.801 --> 00:00:17.225
The argument is short.

6
00:00:17.225 --> 00:00:20.707
Almost every connection
starts with a DNS query,

7
00:00:20.707 --> 00:00:24.363
and the answer now decides
far more than an address.

8
00:00:24.363 --> 00:00:28.731
Most agencies still run DNS across
several platforms that share nothing,

9
00:00:28.731 --> 00:00:33.601
so the same name gets different answers,
and the failures look like something else.

10
00:00:33.601 --> 00:00:37.900
The fix is one authoritative
policy plane, with named owners.

11
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.137
I'll take four questions:
why, what, who and how.

12
00:00:41.137 --> 00:00:45.513
Each decision slide names the decision,
its owner and when it's needed.

13
00:00:45.513 --> 00:00:48.280
Let's start with what DNS now does.

14
00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:51.912
DNS was built to turn names into numbers,

15
00:00:51.912 --> 00:00:56.192
in a world where inside and
outside were separated by a wire.

16
00:00:56.192 --> 00:00:57.518
That world has gone.

17
00:00:57.518 --> 00:01:02.281
Today the answer to a DNS query decides
whether cloud traffic stays private,

18
00:01:02.281 --> 00:01:06.970
which SaaS front door a user reaches,
whether a malicious domain is blocked,

19
00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:09.617
and whether a certificate check succeeds.

20
00:01:09.617 --> 00:01:13.317
It's also the name a Zero Trust
policy is written against.

21
00:01:13.317 --> 00:01:16.181
NIST's 2026 guide says it plainly:

22
00:01:16.181 --> 00:01:20.317
DNS is now a security
control, not just a service.

23
00:01:20.317 --> 00:01:24.008
So the first point for this
room is that governing DNS is

24
00:01:24.008 --> 00:01:27.780
a security and architecture
job, not an operations chore.

25
00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.660
Next, what happens when it isn't governed.

26
00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:35.510
Nobody chooses to run DNS in five places.

27
00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.811
Each cloud ships its own DNS service,

28
00:01:37.811 --> 00:01:42.260
and none of them shares state with
Active Directory or with each other.

29
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:46.672
So the same name drifts into different
answers depending on where the query starts.

30
00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:50.781
That's split-brain, and it fails
quietly: the name still resolves,

31
00:01:50.781 --> 00:01:55.997
just to the wrong place, and it shows up as
a certificate error or an access denial.

32
00:01:55.997 --> 00:01:58.547
Runbooks can't keep five systems in step,

33
00:01:58.547 --> 00:02:03.260
and your real security posture is the
weakest resolver a client can reach.

34
00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.843
So the decision for the sponsor and CIO:

35
00:02:05.843 --> 00:02:10.848
authorize one authoritative policy
plane, before the next migration wave.

36
00:02:10.848 --> 00:02:13.800
Next, what federal policy requires.

37
00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.575
Two requirements shape everything.

38
00:02:16.575 --> 00:02:22.242
M-22-09 says agencies must encrypt DNS
wherever it's technically supported,

39
00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:27.288
and point devices at agency-designated
resolvers, not whatever they discover.

40
00:02:27.288 --> 00:02:32.237
And CISA's Protective DNS is
mandatory for all outbound resolution.

41
00:02:32.237 --> 00:02:34.465
The two fit together: encrypt,

42
00:02:34.465 --> 00:02:39.013
but end at your own resolvers,
with Protective DNS upstream.

43
00:02:39.013 --> 00:02:40.925
Where it breaks is the device.

44
00:02:40.925 --> 00:02:45.780
Encrypted DNS that rides the ordinary
web port can't be seen by the network,

45
00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.600
so it has to be governed through
device and browser policy.

46
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.840
The decision belongs to the CISO:

47
00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.600
designate the resolvers and
the allowed transports, now.

48
00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.600
An exception with no end date
goes to the authorizing official.

49
00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:01.860
Next, the rest of the floor.

50
00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:04.048
Logging changed this year.

51
00:03:04.048 --> 00:03:11.561
OMB's May 2026 memo, M-26-14,
replaced the old logging tiers.

52
00:03:11.561 --> 00:03:16.699
It doesn't name DNS at all; it sets a
baseline, logs searchable for six months

53
00:03:16.699 --> 00:03:21.585
and kept for a year, and leaves the
sources to each agency's logging plan.

54
00:03:21.585 --> 00:03:27.160
That plan is due by November 18, a date
we computed from CISA's publication.

55
00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:31.532
DNS query logs tell you which
address asked for a bad domain;

56
00:03:31.532 --> 00:03:34.923
DHCP lease logs tell you
which device that was.

57
00:03:34.923 --> 00:03:37.222
So the CISO puts both in the plan.

58
00:03:37.222 --> 00:03:43.473
DNSSEC and the IPv6 targets are older
requirements, and the IPv6 dates have passed.

59
00:03:43.473 --> 00:03:47.360
Now, what has to change,
starting with Active Directory.

60
00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:51.502
For twenty years Active
Directory quietly ran your DNS.

61
00:03:51.502 --> 00:03:54.377
Domain controllers host
the zones and publish

62
00:03:54.377 --> 00:03:56.810
the records that let machines find them.

63
00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:58.823
None of that moves to Entra ID.

64
00:03:58.823 --> 00:04:00.721
Entra ID hosts no zones at all,

65
00:04:00.721 --> 00:04:05.072
and some systems will keep needing
domain controllers for a while.

66
00:04:05.072 --> 00:04:08.074
Here's the trap: once a
domain controller is gone,

67
00:04:08.074 --> 00:04:10.922
the records it kept can't
be recovered from it.

68
00:04:10.922 --> 00:04:15.998
So leaving AD DNS is an inventory
problem before it's a migration problem.

69
00:04:15.998 --> 00:04:21.173
The decision: the DNS and DDI team
owns the inventory, AD administrators

70
00:04:21.173 --> 00:04:26.348
do the work, and it's reconciled before
the first domain controller goes.

71
00:04:26.348 --> 00:04:29.780
Next, how queries slip past your controls.

72
00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:33.492
Every control you buy
assumes the query reaches it.

73
00:04:33.492 --> 00:04:38.243
Three ordinary things decide whether it
does, and none of them raises an alarm.

74
00:04:38.243 --> 00:04:43.256
A branch network edge can redirect DNS
to a resolver outside your control.

75
00:04:43.256 --> 00:04:48.530
A device or browser can send encrypted
DNS straight to a public service.

76
00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:54.168
And a rogue DHCP server can hand a whole
subnet an attacker's resolver in one step.

77
00:04:54.168 --> 00:04:56.693
So the decision splits
deciding from doing.

78
00:04:56.693 --> 00:05:02.255
The DNS and DDI team decides that every
edge points only at agency resolvers;

79
00:05:02.255 --> 00:05:07.817
network engineering configures it; and
the CISO owns rogue DHCP detection.

80
00:05:07.817 --> 00:05:11.830
Do it as authority moves, then
check before every change.

81
00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:13.930
Next, certificates.

82
00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:18.294
A certificate can be valid
and unrevoked and still fail,

83
00:05:18.294 --> 00:05:22.930
because every check that makes it
trustworthy starts with a DNS lookup.

84
00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:27.751
If the revocation names live only in
Active Directory DNS, a cloud workload

85
00:05:27.751 --> 00:05:32.105
can't reach them, and the ticket gets
opened as a certificate problem.

86
00:05:32.105 --> 00:05:35.680
The same goes for sign-in
endpoints and wildcard names.

87
00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.167
DNSSEC protects the answers themselves.

88
00:05:38.167 --> 00:05:41.668
The program's position: sign
your external zones always,

89
00:05:41.668 --> 00:05:46.918
validate on resolvers you control, and sign
internal zones only where it's required.

90
00:05:46.918 --> 00:05:48.380
The CISO sets that.

91
00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:52.143
And moving a zone somewhere it can't
be signed is a risk acceptance,

92
00:05:52.143 --> 00:05:54.442
so the authorizing official decides it.

93
00:05:54.442 --> 00:05:56.210
Next, addresses.

94
00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.085
Names and addresses are the same problem.

95
00:05:59.085 --> 00:06:01.673
Every cloud has its own address manager,

96
00:06:01.673 --> 00:06:05.360
and left alone each one hands
out address space on its own.

97
00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.462
You find the overlap when
two networks are joined,

98
00:06:08.462 --> 00:06:11.110
or an auditor finds space nobody recorded.

99
00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:14.898
So the enterprise IPAM is the
authority for address blocks.

100
00:06:14.898 --> 00:06:17.383
Some platforms integrate natively;

101
00:06:17.383 --> 00:06:21.111
others need you to reserve
first, mirror, and reconcile.

102
00:06:21.111 --> 00:06:23.847
Either way it becomes the address layer of

103
00:06:23.847 --> 00:06:26.585
the asset inventory Zero Trust depends on.

104
00:06:26.585 --> 00:06:30.445
The decision: the DNS and DDI
team owns the address plan,

105
00:06:30.445 --> 00:06:35.335
and every new cloud network is
reserved in IPAM before it's created.

106
00:06:35.335 --> 00:06:37.530
Next, the architecture.

107
00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:40.255
The architecture is simple to draw.

108
00:06:40.255 --> 00:06:44.805
Branch edge, then an agency resolver,
then the policy plane, then each

109
00:06:44.805 --> 00:06:50.005
cloud's private zones, with internet
queries leaving through Protective DNS.

110
00:06:50.005 --> 00:06:55.294
The risk sits in five settings: where the
edge points, which resolver goes first,

111
00:06:55.294 --> 00:06:59.037
where cloud forwarding rules
live, the AD DNS inventory,

112
00:06:59.037 --> 00:07:02.780
and whether revocation names
resolve from everywhere.

113
00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:04.052
Each fails silently,

114
00:07:04.052 --> 00:07:07.868
so each needs a check in the live
configuration, not the design.

115
00:07:07.868 --> 00:07:09.547
The book's ask is specific:

116
00:07:09.547 --> 00:07:14.905
the DNS and DDI team settles all five within
ninety days of authorizing the plane,

117
00:07:14.905 --> 00:07:18.904
and reports the remaining risk
to the authorizing official.

118
00:07:18.904 --> 00:07:21.270
Next, choosing the platform.

119
00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:26.095
The platform decision is really a
decision about the source of truth.

120
00:07:26.095 --> 00:07:29.245
One plane holds the zones,
the forwarding rules,

121
00:07:29.245 --> 00:07:34.495
the address data and the security policy,
and the cloud DNS services consume it.

122
00:07:34.495 --> 00:07:37.639
There are three models:
a SaaS policy plane,

123
00:07:37.639 --> 00:07:42.670
DDI the agency runs itself, or
cloud-native services managed as code.

124
00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:47.707
Each has a trade-off, and a small
single-cloud agency may need only the last.

125
00:07:47.707 --> 00:07:50.312
Remember that FedRAMP certifies a service,

126
00:07:50.312 --> 00:07:53.133
not your system, and
Moderate doesn't cover High.

127
00:07:53.133 --> 00:07:56.928
So the sponsor and CIO choose on
evidence from your own estate,

128
00:07:56.928 --> 00:08:01.170
and the authorizing official
authorizes any cloud-delivered plane.

129
00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:02.730
Now, who owns it.

130
00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:05.645
In an Active Directory estate,

131
00:08:05.645 --> 00:08:09.630
nobody wrote down who owned
DNS, because AD did it for you.

132
00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:12.084
One admin group controlled every zone,

133
00:08:12.084 --> 00:08:15.305
and the domain controller
team decided by default.

134
00:08:15.305 --> 00:08:18.503
A single policy plane
concentrates that power:

135
00:08:18.503 --> 00:08:23.342
one team can now break resolution for
every user and every cloud at once.

136
00:08:23.342 --> 00:08:27.398
So owners have to be named on
purpose, one decider per decision,

137
00:08:27.398 --> 00:08:31.931
or the cloud, network and directory
teams each own a piece by accident.

138
00:08:31.931 --> 00:08:34.580
Two decisions before
the transition starts.

139
00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:38.730
The sponsor and CIO give the
decision rights to a named group.

140
00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:44.430
And AD administrators strip the DNS admin
group down to domain administrators.

141
00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:46.500
Next, the matrix.

142
00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.188
Here's the matrix in one view.

143
00:08:49.188 --> 00:08:54.026
The sponsor and CIO decide the platform
and when the old services retire.

144
00:08:54.026 --> 00:08:57.713
The CISO decides encryption,
DNSSEC and logging.

145
00:08:57.713 --> 00:09:00.864
The DNS and DDI team
decides the namespace,

146
00:09:00.864 --> 00:09:04.737
the edge target, the forwarding
and the address plan.

147
00:09:04.737 --> 00:09:10.150
Network engineering and AD administrators
do much of the work, but don't decide it.

148
00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:12.925
Why does the authorizing
official appear twice?

149
00:09:12.925 --> 00:09:16.850
Because many DNS changes are
risk decisions in disguise.

150
00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:21.587
Pointing an edge at a public resolver
quietly turns off Protective DNS.

151
00:09:21.587 --> 00:09:25.589
So every standing exception goes
to the authorizing official,

152
00:09:25.589 --> 00:09:27.513
with an owner and a review date.

153
00:09:27.513 --> 00:09:29.880
Next, everyday changes.

154
00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:33.543
Most DNS changes don't
need a change board.

155
00:09:33.543 --> 00:09:36.627
Treat them all alike and
you get either a six-week

156
00:09:36.627 --> 00:09:39.404
wait for a simple record,
or no control at all.

157
00:09:39.404 --> 00:09:41.855
So the program uses three classes.

158
00:09:41.855 --> 00:09:46.455
Standard changes inside a delegated
namespace are approved in advance.

159
00:09:46.455 --> 00:09:52.043
Records written by a policy need no approval
each time, but the policy itself does.

160
00:09:52.043 --> 00:09:57.317
And high-impact changes, like zones,
forwarding and wildcards, go to the board.

161
00:09:57.317 --> 00:10:01.327
One more point: the account the
plane uses to write into each

162
00:10:01.327 --> 00:10:05.418
cloud is the most powerful identity
in the design, so guard it.

163
00:10:05.418 --> 00:10:10.917
The DNS and DDI team adopts the classes
before namespaces are delegated.

164
00:10:10.917 --> 00:10:12.660
Now, how to migrate.

165
00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:19.373
The migration runs in six phases:
inventory, coexist, move authority,

166
00:10:19.373 --> 00:10:25.123
bring in the clouds, retire AD DNS
and DHCP, then harden and prove it.

167
00:10:25.123 --> 00:10:28.889
Name resolution has to keep
working while authority moves,

168
00:10:28.889 --> 00:10:31.272
so every move runs the same small loop.

169
00:10:31.272 --> 00:10:36.072
The old system keeps answering until the
new one is proven from every location,

170
00:10:36.072 --> 00:10:38.361
and a failed check just switches back.

171
00:10:38.361 --> 00:10:42.775
The program has planning estimates,
from a day or two up to a few weeks,

172
00:10:42.775 --> 00:10:45.298
but they're estimates, not commitments.

173
00:10:45.298 --> 00:10:48.838
The decision: the DNS
and DDI team closes each

174
00:10:48.838 --> 00:10:52.473
phase on its evidence,
never on a calendar date.

175
00:10:52.473 --> 00:10:54.990
Next, the step you can't undo.

176
00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:59.077
This is where DNS meets
the identity program.

177
00:10:59.077 --> 00:11:04.121
While any domain controller remains, it
needs DNS that accepts its registrations,

178
00:11:04.121 --> 00:11:06.716
so those records are
the last thing to move.

179
00:11:06.716 --> 00:11:10.941
Retiring AD DNS is the one
step you can't cheaply undo,

180
00:11:10.941 --> 00:11:13.103
so it's gated, not scheduled.

181
00:11:13.103 --> 00:11:16.954
Five gates, all program design:
the inventory reconciles;

182
00:11:16.954 --> 00:11:22.525
the critical records resolve everywhere
for thirty days; ninety days of parallel

183
00:11:22.525 --> 00:11:27.441
running; failover actually tested; and
no traffic left on the old servers.

184
00:11:27.441 --> 00:11:30.890
Get it wrong and you get
forest-wide sign-in failures.

185
00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:34.959
So the sponsor and CIO set the
date only after every gate passes,

186
00:11:34.959 --> 00:11:37.228
with the records officer involved.

187
00:11:37.228 --> 00:11:38.880
Next, running it.

188
00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:42.673
One plane only helps if
divisions can still run their

189
00:11:42.673 --> 00:11:45.855
own networks through it, and
every change leaves a trace.

190
00:11:45.855 --> 00:11:49.348
So divisions get scoped
control, and every change,

191
00:11:49.348 --> 00:11:54.205
by a person or a script, goes through
one interface and one audit trail.

192
00:11:54.205 --> 00:11:59.405
The logs feed security operations, the
inventory and the authorization package.

193
00:11:59.405 --> 00:12:03.120
And measure one thing first:
resolution-path consistency,

194
00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:07.455
meaning the same name gets the
intended answer from every location.

195
00:12:07.455 --> 00:12:10.468
Baseline it now, set a
target, and report it.

196
00:12:10.468 --> 00:12:15.328
The book doesn't say who reports, so in
our model it's the DNS and DDI team,

197
00:12:15.328 --> 00:12:18.142
reporting to the authorizing official.

198
00:12:18.142 --> 00:12:20.340
Next, cost and risk.

199
00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:23.777
Commercial DDI licensing isn't published,

200
00:12:23.777 --> 00:12:27.540
so get quotes from every
candidate on the same assumptions.

201
00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:31.540
The cloud pieces do have public
prices, and every model uses them.

202
00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:36.193
One region's cloud resolver hub runs about
three hundred and sixty dollars a month

203
00:12:36.193 --> 00:12:39.888
before zones and queries, and the
book corrects earlier guidance

204
00:12:39.888 --> 00:12:43.515
that got the pricing backwards, so
re-check any budget built on it.

205
00:12:43.515 --> 00:12:48.511
Some risk remains after the plane is
in: sync lag, a management outage that

206
00:12:48.511 --> 00:12:54.090
blocks changes, encrypted DNS bypass, and
the limits of a Moderate authorization.

207
00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:59.415
The sponsor and CIO handle this before
selection, and record the residual risk.

208
00:12:59.415 --> 00:13:01.640
Let's pull the decisions together.

209
00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:03.965
So, the decisions.

210
00:13:03.965 --> 00:13:09.864
The sponsor and CIO authorize one policy
plane, choose the platform on evidence,

211
00:13:09.864 --> 00:13:14.690
name the decision rights, and retire
AD DNS only through the gates.

212
00:13:14.690 --> 00:13:18.163
The CISO designates the
encrypted resolvers now,

213
00:13:18.163 --> 00:13:23.123
puts DNS and DHCP logs in the logging
plan by November 18, and sets signing

214
00:13:23.123 --> 00:13:28.415
and validation, with standing exceptions
going to the authorizing official.

215
00:13:28.415 --> 00:13:34.402
The DNS and DDI team reconciles the AD
inventory before any domain controller goes,

216
00:13:34.402 --> 00:13:39.609
aims every edge at agency resolvers,
makes IPAM the address authority,

217
00:13:39.609 --> 00:13:43.253
and settles the five
decisions within ninety days.

218
00:13:43.253 --> 00:13:46.865
Govern the name layer before
the last domain controller goes.

219
00:13:46.865 --> 00:13:50.940
Book 08 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

220
00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:51.760
Thank you.
